['Here's a simple C program that fulfills these requirements. It reads integers from a file one at a time, keeping track of the largest integer seen so far. If there are fewer than 10 integers in the file, it prints "Insufficient data" and terminates. This solution is efficient with memory usage as it only keeps track of the current maximum value and does not store all values in memory.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void process_file(const char *filename) {
    FILE *fp = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open file\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    int max_val;
    int count = 0;
    while (!feof(fp)) {
        int val;
        fscanf(fp, "%d", &val);

        if (count == 0) {
            max_val = val;
        } else {
            if (val > max_val) {
                max_val = val;
            }
        }

        count++;
    }

    fclose(fp);

    if (count < 10) {
        printf("Insufficient data\n");
    } else {
        printf("Largest integer: %d\n", max_val);
    }
}
```
This program uses `fscanf` to read integers from the file one at a time, which minimizes memory usage. It keeps track of the maximum value seen so far and updates it as necessary. If there are fewer than 10 integers in the file, it prints "Insufficient data". The function `feof(fp)` is used to check if we have reached the end of the file.

This solution assumes that the integers in the file are separated by whitespace and that they fit within an int. If this is not the case, additional error checking would be needed. 

The program uses `fopen` to open the file for reading, which should work on any system where C standard library functions are supported. The function `exit(1)` is used to terminate the program if it fails to open the file, as this is a common way of handling errors in C programs.
']

