['Here's a basic implementation in C++ that meets your requirements. This version uses std::vector to store the list of integers, which is a dynamic array type provided by the Standard Template Library (STL). It also includes error handling for empty lists and non-integer values. 

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

int search(std::vector<int>& list, int target, bool binarySearch = false) {
    if (list.empty()) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("List is empty");
    }

    for (const auto &item : list) {
        if (!(item >= -1000 && item <= 1000)) { // assuming integers are in the range of [-1000, 1000]
            throw std::invalid_argument("List contains non-integer values");
        }
    }

    if (binarySearch) {
        if (!std::is_sorted(list.begin(), list.end())) {
            std::sort(list.begin(), list.end());
        }

        auto it = std::lower_bound(list.begin(), list.end(), target);
        if (it != list.end() && *it == target) {
            return static_cast<int>(std::distance(list.begin(), it));
        } else {
            return -1;
        }
    } else {
        auto it = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), target);
        if (it != list.end()) {
            return static_cast<int>(std::distance(list.begin(), it));
        } else {
            return -1;
        }
    }
}
```
This function first checks whether the list is empty or contains non-integer values, and throws an exception if either of these conditions are met. If binary search mode is enabled and the list is not sorted, it sorts the list in ascending order before performing the search. It then uses std::lower_bound for binary search (which returns a pointer to the first element that is not less than target) or std::find for linear search.

Please note that this function assumes integers are within the range of [-1000, 1000]. If you have different ranges or need to handle larger numbers, you may need to adjust the validation check accordingly. Also, please be aware that sorting a large list can be computationally expensive and might not be suitable for very large datasets.
']

