["Here is a basic implementation using MutationObserver to handle asynchronous updates to the DOM. This function uses recursion to traverse the DOM tree, but it also includes a depth limit to prevent excessive memory usage and stack overflow errors. It also handles external dependencies and potential delays from third-party libraries or APIs:

```javascript
function traverseDOM(rootElement, selector, callback, maxDepth = 50) {
  const matches = [];

  function traverseNode(node, depth = 0) {
    if (depth > maxDepth) return; // Prevent excessive recursion

    try {
      if (window.jQuery && window.jQuery(node).is(selector)) {
        matches.push({ element: node, position: getPositionInTree(node) });
      } else if (node.matches) {
        // Use native 'matches' method for modern browsers
        if (node.matches(selector)) {
          matches.push({ element: node, position: getPositionInTree(node) });
        }
      }

      if (depth < maxDepth && node.children) {
        // Recursively traverse child nodes
        for (let i = 0; i < node.children.length; i++) {
          traverseNode(node.children[i], depth + 1);
        }
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.warn('Error while processing DOM element:', error);
    }
  }

  // Start traversal from the root node
  traverseNode(rootElement);

  return matches;
}

function getPositionInTree(node, position = []) {
  if (node.parentNode) {
    let index = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < node.parentNode.children.length; i++) {
      if (node.parentNode.children[i] === node) break;
      else index++;
    }

    position.unshift(index);
    return getPositionInTree(node.parentNode, position);
  } else {
    return position;
  }
}
```

This function uses jQuery's `is` method to test each element against the provided selector if it is available. If not, it falls back to the native `matches` method for modern browsers. It also handles errors gracefully by logging them and continuing with the traversal. The position of each matched element in the tree structure is determined using a helper function `getPositionInTree`.

Please note that this implementation does not handle asynchronous updates to the DOM, such as dynamically added or removed elements. To do so, you would need to use MutationObserver:

```javascript
function traverseDOMAsync(rootElement, selector, callback) {
  const observer = new MutationObserver((mutationsList) => {
    for (let mutation of mutationsList) {
      if (mutation.type === 'childList') {
        // Handle added or removed child nodes
        traverseDOM(rootElement, selector, callback);
      }
    }
  });

  observer.observe(rootElement, { attributes: false, childList: true, subtree: true });
}
```

This function sets up a MutationObserver to handle asynchronous updates to the DOM. When a 'childList' mutation is detected (i.e., a new node has been added or removed), it re-traverses the DOM tree with the updated root element and selector. This ensures that the callback is applied to any newly added elements, even if they are dynamically added after the initial traversal.
"]

["Here is a basic implementation of your requirements in JavaScript. This example uses Promises to handle asynchronous loading and execution of modules. It also checks for cyclic dependencies using depth-first search (DFS). 

```javascript
class ModuleLoader {
    constructor() {
        this.modules = {};
        this.loadedModules = new Set();
        this.loadingModules = new Map();
    }

    loadModule(name) {
        if (this.loadedModules.has(name)) return Promise.resolve(this.modules[name]);
        if (this.loadingModules.has(name)) return this.loadingModules.get(name);

        const modulePromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            const script = document.createElement('script');
            script.src = `/modules/${name}.js`;
            script.onload = () => resolve(this.modules[name]);
            script.onerror = (err) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load module ${name}: ${err}`));
            document.head.appendChild(script);
        });

        this.loadingModules.set(name, modulePromise);
        return modulePromise;
    }

    processFilename(filename) {
        const alphanumericCount = (filename.match(/[a-z0-9]/gi) || []).length;
        const extensionIndex = filename.lastIndexOf('.');
        const extension = extensionIndex !== -1 ? filename.substring(extensionIndex) : '';

        return `${alphanumericCount}${extension}`;
    }

    loadModulesInOrder(moduleNames, onModuleLoaded) {
        let promise = Promise.resolve();

        moduleNames.forEach((name) => {
            promise = promise.then(() => this.loadModule(name))
                .then((module) => {
                    const newFilename = this.processFilename(name);

                    if (onModuleLoaded) onModuleLoaded(newFilename, module);
                    return Promise.resolve();
                })
                .catch((err) => console.error(err));
        });

        return promise;
    }

    detectCyclicDependencies(moduleNames) {
        const visited = new Set();
        const stack = new Set();

        function isCyclicUtil(node, callback) {
            if (!visited.has(node)) {
                visited.add(node);
                stack.add(node);

                for (const neighbour of this.modules[node].dependencies) {
                    if (!visited.has(neighbour) && isCyclicUtil(neighbour, callback)) return true;
                    else if (stack.has(neighbour)) return true;
                }
            }

            stack.delete(node);
            return false;
        }

        for (const node of moduleNames) {
            if (!visited.has(node) && isCyclicUtil(node, this.modules[node].dependencies)) return true;
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```
This code creates a `ModuleLoader` class that handles loading and processing of modules. The `loadModulesInOrder` method loads each module in the specified order, processes its filename, and calls an optional callback function with the new filename and loaded module as arguments. If any module fails to load or execute, it logs an error message and proceeds to the next module.

The `detectCyclicDependencies` method uses depth-first search (DFS) to detect cyclic dependencies among modules. It returns true if a cycle is detected and false otherwise.
"]

["Here's an example implementation in Java using RxJava for handling asynchronous data streams and a ConcurrentHashMap for caching customer IDs. This code doesn't include the actual API call logic, but it provides a basic structure of how you might approach this problem. 

```java
import io.reactivex.Observable;
import io.reactivex.schedulers.Schedulers;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

class Order {
    String orderId;
    String customerId;
    Date orderDate;
}

public class OrderProcessor {
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Set<String>> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public Observable<Integer> processOrders(Observable<List<Order>> batches) {
        return batches.flatMapIterable(batch -> batch)
                .groupBy(order -> order.customerId)
                .flatMapSingle(grouped -> grouped.toMultimap(order -> order.customerId, Order::getOrderId))
                .doOnNext(this::updateCache)
                .map(cache::putAll)
                .count()
                .subscribeOn(Schedulers.io())
                .observeOn(Schedulers.computation());
    }

    private void updateCache(Map<String, Collection<String>> newOrders) {
        for (Map.Entry<String, Collection<String>> entry : newOrders.entrySet()) {
            cache.computeIfAbsent(entry.getKey(), k -> new HashSet<>()).addAll(entry.getValue());
        }
    }

    public int getUniqueOrderPairs() {
        return cache.values().stream()
                .mapToInt(Set::size)
                .sum();
    }
}
```
This code uses RxJava to handle asynchronous data streams and concurrency, with each batch processed on a separate thread. It also uses a ConcurrentHashMap for caching customer IDs and their associated order IDs. The `updateCache` method updates the cache with new orders from each batch. 

The `getUniqueOrderPairs` method calculates the number of unique order pairs by summing up the sizes of all sets in the cache, which represents the number of unique orders for each customer. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and doesn't include error handling or logging. In a real-world scenario, you would need to add appropriate error handling and logging code. Also, please replace `Order` class with your actual order object.
