['Here is a basic example of how you might implement this functionality in C using POSIX threads (pthreads) for thread safety, and dlopen/dlsym to load the shared library. This code does not include error checking or handling for all possible failure scenarios, but it should give you a good starting point:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    double result;
} ResultStruct;

void* load_and_compute(void *arg) {
    char *config_file = (char*) arg;

    void *handle = dlopen("./libmath.so", RTLD_LAZY);
    if (!handle) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to load shared library: %s\n", dlerror());
        return NULL;
    }

    typedef ResultStruct* (*compute_func)(char *config_file);
    compute_func func = (compute_func)dlsym(handle, "compute");
    char *error = dlerror();
    if (error != NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to find 'compute' function: %s\n", error);
        return NULL;
    }

    ResultStruct *result = func(config_file);

    dlclose(handle);

    return result;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t thread1, thread2;
    char *config_files[] = {"config1.txt", "config2.txt"};

    if (pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, load_and_compute, config_files[0])) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error creating thread\n");
        return 1;
    }

    if (pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, load_and_compute, config_files[1])) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error creating thread\n");
        return 1;
    }

    ResultStruct *result1, *result2;
    pthread_join(thread1, (void**)&result1);
    pthread_join(thread2, (void**)&result2);

    printf("Result from thread 1: %f\n", result1->result);
    printf("Result from thread 2: %f\n", result2->result);

    free(result1);
    free(result2);

    return 0;
}
```

This code assumes that the shared library provides a function named "compute" with the following signature: `ResultStruct* compute(char *config_file)`. It also assumes that the ResultStruct is dynamically allocated and needs to be freed by the caller.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all of the requirements or error checking you would need in a production environment. For instance, it doesn't handle concurrent updates to the shared library, nor does it provide any mechanism for ensuring thread safety when multiple threads are using the same shared library at the same time.
']

["Here is a basic implementation in C that follows your requirements. This code does not include error checking or handling of edge cases such as self-loops and multiple transitions to the same state. It also assumes that the memory allocation function provided by the user always returns valid pointers, which may not be true in all scenarios:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct State {
    int index;
    struct State* next;
} State;

typedef struct FSA {
    int** transitionTable;
    int startState;
    int numStates;
} FSA;

typedef State* (*MemoryAllocator)(size_t);

State* constructSequence(int* initialList, int listSize, FSA* fsa, int target, int* totalProcessed, int maxTime, MemoryAllocator alloc) {
    clock_t start = clock();
    *totalProcessed = 0;

    State* head = NULL;
    State** tail = &head;

    for (int i = 0; i < listSize; ++i) {
        int currentState = initialList[i];

        if (currentState < 0 || currentState >= fsa->numStates) {
            // Invalid state index, skip it
            continue;
        }

        while (*totalProcessed < maxTime && currentState != target) {
            State* newState = alloc(sizeof(State));

            if (newState == NULL) {
                // Insufficient memory, return NULL
                return NULL;
            }

            newState->index = currentState;
            newState->next = NULL;

            *tail = newState;
            tail = &(newState->next);

            currentState = fsa->transitionTable[currentState][0]; // Assuming deterministic transitions
            ++(*totalProcessed);
        }

        if (currentState == target) {
            State* newState = alloc(sizeof(State));

            if (newState == NULL) {
                // Insufficient memory, return NULL
                return NULL;
            }

            newState->index = currentState;
            newState->next = NULL;

            *tail = newState;
        } else {
            // Maximum processing time exceeded, return NULL
            return NULL;
        }
    }

    return head;
}
```
This function constructs a sequence of states from the provided FSA by following transitions starting from each state in the initial list. It uses a linked list to store the constructed sequence and updates the 'next' pointers as it goes along. The function also keeps track of the total number of states processed during construction, checks for insufficient memory, and handles edge cases where the initial state list is empty or contains invalid state indices.
"]

['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements in Rust. This code does not cover all edge cases, but it should give you a good starting point. 

