["Here is an example of how you might implement this in C using a linked list approach for managing network entities. This code does not include all the features mentioned such as fetching data from external source, caching mechanism, thread-safe operations etc. These are complex topics and would require a lot more code to fully cover them.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct NetworkEntity {
    int id;
    int statusFlags;
    float performanceMetrics[2]; // Assuming we have 2 metrics for simplicity
    struct NetworkEntity *next;
} NetworkEntity;

NetworkEntity* createEntity(int id, int flags) {
    NetworkEntity* entity = (NetworkEntity*) malloc(sizeof(NetworkEntity));
    if(entity == NULL) return NULL; // Out of memory

    entity->id = id;
    entity->statusFlags = flags;
    entity->next = NULL;
    return entity;
}

void destroyEntity(NetworkEntity* entity) {
    free(entity);
}

typedef struct NetworkList {
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    NetworkEntity *head;
} NetworkList;

void initList(NetworkList* list) {
    list->head = NULL;
    pthread_mutex_init(&list->lock, NULL);
}

void destroyList(NetworkList* list) {
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&list->lock);

    NetworkEntity *current = list->head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        NetworkEntity *next = current->next;
        destroyEntity(current);
        current = next;
    }
}

void addToList(NetworkList* list, NetworkEntity* entity) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list->lock);

    if (list->head == NULL) {
        list->head = entity;
    } else {
        NetworkEntity *current = list->head;
        while(current->next != NULL) current = current->next;
        current->next = entity;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list->lock);
}
```
This code uses a linked list to manage the network entities. Each entity is dynamically allocated and deallocated when it's no longer needed, minimizing memory fragmentation. The use of mutexes ensures that concurrent updates from multiple threads are safe. 

However, this code does not include fetching data from an external source or caching mechanism for performance improvement. These features would require additional complexity and could be implemented using techniques such as threading, locks, condition variables, and possibly a cache system.

The trade-offs between lock-free structures and traditional mutexes are complex and depend on the specific use case. Traditional mutexes provide predictable behavior and can be easier to reason about, but they may introduce contention overhead if many threads try to acquire the same lock concurrently. Lock-free data structures, on the other hand, can offer better performance in certain scenarios by avoiding locks entirely, but correctness is more difficult to achieve and there are potential issues with ABA problems.
"]

