["Here is a basic implementation in C using pthreads for thread safety. This code does not include all the considerations you mentioned due to their complexity, but it provides a good starting point. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int sid;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    int packetCount;
} Session;

typedef struct {
    int pid;
    time_t timestamp;
    size_t payloadSize;
} Packet;

Session* sessions = NULL;
int sessionCapacity = 0;
pthread_mutex_t globalLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void initSessions(int N, int M) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&globalLock);

    sessions = (Session*)calloc(N, sizeof(Session));
    sessionCapacity = N;

    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        sessions[i].sid = i + 1;
        pthread_mutex_init(&sessions[i].lock, NULL);
        sessions[i].packetCount = 0;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&globalLock);
}

int addPacket(int sid, Packet* packet) {
    if (sid < 1 || sid > sessionCapacity) return -1; // Invalid SID

    Session* session = &sessions[sid - 1];
    pthread_mutex_lock(&session->lock);

    if (session->packetCount >= M) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&session->lock);
        return -2; // Session is full
    }

    session->packetCount++;
    packet->pid = session->packetCount;
    packet->timestamp = time(NULL);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&session->lock);

    return 0; // Success
}
```
This code initializes an array of sessions with a given capacity N. Each session has its own lock to ensure thread safety when adding packets. The `addPacket` function adds a packet to the specified session, returning -1 if the SID is invalid and -2 if the session is full. 

Please note that this code does not handle errors or edge cases in a robust manner. It's intended as a starting point for further development. For example, it doesn't handle memory allocation failures, nor does it provide any mechanism to remove sessions or packets once added. You would need to add these features yourself based on your specific requirements and constraints.
