["Here's an example of how you might implement such a module in C. This code does not include all the features mentioned above due to its complexity but it provides a basic structure that can be expanded upon. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

typedef struct {
    sem_t *sem;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
} Semaphore;

Semaphore* create_semaphore(int value) {
    Semaphore *s = malloc(sizeof(Semaphore));
    if (s == NULL) return NULL;

    s->sem = malloc(sizeof(sem_t));
    if (s->sem == NULL) {
        free(s);
        return NULL;
    }

    if (pthread_mutex_init(&s->mutex, NULL) != 0) {
        free(s->sem);
        free(s);
        return NULL;
    }

    if (sem_init(s->sem, 0, value) != 0) {
        pthread_mutex_destroy(&s->mutex);
        free(s->sem);
        free(s);
        return NULL;
    }

    return s;
}

void delete_semaphore(Semaphore *s) {
    if (s == NULL) return;

    sem_destroy(s->sem);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&s->mutex);
    free(s->sem);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&s->mutex);
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&s->mutex);
    free(s);
}

void semaphore_wait(Semaphore *s) {
    if (s == NULL) return;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&s->mutex);
    while (sem_trywait(s->sem) != 0) {
        pthread_yield();
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&s->mutex);
}

void semaphore_signal(Semaphore *s) {
    if (s == NULL) return;

    sem_post(s->sem);
}
```
This code creates a `Semaphore` structure that encapsulates a POSIX semaphore and a mutex. The `create_semaphore` function allocates memory for the semaphore and initializes it with a specified value, while the `delete_semaphore` function frees all allocated resources.

The `semaphore_wait` and `semaphore_signal` functions are used to decrement and increment the semaphore's value respectively. They use a spinlock to wait for the semaphore to become available before attempting to decrement it, which can help avoid contention under high concurrency.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all of the features mentioned in your requirements. It also assumes that you have an external resource manager that can provide memory allocation services. You would need to integrate with this manager to handle potential failures and manage its capacity limits. 

Also, error handling is minimal and only checks for NULL pointers and failed system calls. More comprehensive error checking and recovery strategies should be implemented in a production environment.
