["Here is an example of how you might implement such a function in JavaScript:

```javascript
class StreamingBuffer {
  constructor() {
    this.buffers = [];
    this.totalBytes = 0;
  }

  addChunk(chunk) {
    if (!Buffer.isBuffer(chunk)) throw new Error('Invalid chunk format');
    this.buffers.push(chunk);
    this.totalBytes += chunk.length;
  }

  copyToNewBuffer(byteCount, callback) {
    if (byteCount > Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER || byteCount < 0) throw new Error('Invalid byte count');
    if (this.totalBytes < byteCount) return callback(new Error('Not enough data'));

    let remaining = byteCount;
    const newBuffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(byteCount);
    let offset = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < this.buffers.length && remaining > 0; i++) {
      const chunk = this.buffers[i];
      if (chunk.length <= remaining) {
        chunk.copy(newBuffer, offset);
        remaining -= chunk.length;
        offset += chunk.length;
      } else {
        chunk.copy(newBuffer, offset, 0, remaining);
        this.buffers[i] = chunk.slice(remaining);
        break;
      }
    }

    callback(null, newBuffer);
  }
}
```
This code creates a `StreamingBuffer` class that manages a list of buffered data chunks and their total size. The `addChunk()` method adds a chunk to the buffer and updates the total byte count. The `copyToNewBuffer()` method copies a specified number of bytes from the buffers into a new buffer, handling edge cases where the requested byte count exceeds the available bytes or is zero.

This implementation assumes that data chunks are added in sequence and do not overlap. If your application requires non-sequential or overlapping data chunks, you would need to modify this code accordingly. 

