['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements using C++14. This code does not include all the features you requested (like integration with an external data source or handling network latency), but it provides a good starting point for understanding how to implement these features. 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <cmath>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

struct Transaction {
    int id;
    std::time_t timestamp;
    double value;
    int categoryId;
};

class TransactionProcessor {
public:
    void processTransaction(const Transaction& transaction) {
        auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mMutex);

        if (mProcessedTransactions.find(transaction.id) != mProcessedTransactions.end()) {
            return;
        }

        mProcessedTransactions[transaction.id] = true;

        double weight = transaction.value * std::sqrt(transaction.categoryId + 1);

        if (weight > mMaxWeight) {
            mMaxWeight = weight;
            mMaxWeightTransactionId = transaction.id;
        }

        mCategoryCounts[transaction.categoryId]++;
        mAverageValues[transaction.categoryId] += transaction.value;

        while (!mTransactions.empty() && now - mTransactions.front().timestamp > std::chrono::seconds(10)) {
            const Transaction& oldTransaction = mTransactions.front();

            mAverageValues[oldTransaction.categoryId] -= oldTransaction.value;
            if (--mCategoryCounts[oldTransaction.categoryId] == 0) {
                mAverageValues.erase(oldTransaction.categoryId);
                mCategoryCounts.erase(oldTransaction.categoryId);
            }

            mTransactions.erase(mTransactions.begin());
        }

        mTransactions.push_back(transaction);
    }

private:
    std::vector<Transaction> mTransactions;
    std::unordered_map<int, bool> mProcessedTransactions;
    std::unordered_map<int, int> mCategoryCounts;
    std::unordered_map<int, double> mAverageValues;
    double mMaxWeight = 0.0;
    int mMaxWeightTransactionId = -1;
    std::mutex mMutex;
};
```
This code uses a sliding window mechanism to keep track of the last 10 seconds of transactions. It also keeps track of the maximum weighted sum and corresponding transaction ID, as well as the average transaction value per category. Duplicate detection is handled efficiently by using an unordered_map to store processed transaction IDs.

Please note that this code does not handle exceptions or network latency issues. For a production-ready system, you would need to add appropriate error handling and potentially use a more robust data structure for the sliding window mechanism (e.g., circular buffer). Also, if you want to scale horizontally, you might need to consider using a distributed computing framework like Apache Spark or Hadoop.
']

