['Here's an example implementation in C using pthread mutexes for thread safety. This code assumes that you have a `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structure defined with the fields `RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG`, `RNTI`, and `CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG`.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

int hashTable[1000]; // Assuming RNTI is in range [0, 999]

void add(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (head == NULL || head->data.RNTI != data.RNTI) {
        Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
        assert(newNode != NULL);

        newNode->data = data;
        newNode->next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }

    hashTable[data.RNTI]++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void report() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        if (hashTable[i] > 0) {
            printf("RNTI: %d, CRC checks: %d\n", i, hashTable[i]);
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses a linked list to store the data and a hash table for fast `RNTI` lookup. It ensures that no two consecutive structures in the list share the same `RNTI` value by checking if the new node's `RNTI` is different from the head of the list. The trade-off here is memory usage, as we are using an array to store the counts for each `RNTI`. This could be a problem if the range of `RNTI` values is very large. However, this solution provides efficient memory management and thread safety.
