['Here is a basic implementation of such a function in C using pthread library for thread safety and custom linked list structure for efficient insertion and retrieval operations. This code does not include the memory monitoring, logging or error handling mechanisms as they would significantly increase the complexity of this example. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct Record {
    int key;
    char* value;
    struct Record *next;
} Record;

Record *head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void insert(int key, const char* value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Record *newNode = (Record*) malloc(sizeof(Record));
    if(newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        exit(-1);
    }

    newNode->key = key;
    newNode->value = strdup(value);
    if(newNode->value == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        free(newNode);
        exit(-1);
    }

    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

char* retrieve(int key) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Record *current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        if (current->key == key) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return current->value;
        }
        current = current->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return NULL;
}
```
This code provides a simple linked list structure for storing records. The `insert` function adds new records to the head of the list, and the `retrieve` function searches through the list from the head until it finds a record with the given key. Both functions are thread-safe by using a mutex lock.

Please note that this is a very basic implementation and does not include many features such as memory monitoring, logging or error handling mechanisms. For production code, you would need to add these features and handle more edge cases. Also, for large number of records, a hash table or balanced search tree might be a better data structure due to its faster access time.
']

