["Here is an example of how you might implement this in PHP using PDO for database operations and Redis for reference counting and handling concurrent access from multiple threads. Please note that the actual implementation would be much more complex and require a lot more code, including error handling, logging, and testing. 

```php
<?php
class UserAccountManager {
    private $db; // PDO instance
    private $redis; // Redis instance

    public function __construct($pdo, $redis) {
        $this->db = $pdo;
        $this->redis = $redis;
    }

    public function manageUserAccounts($request) {
        if (!isset($request['name']) || !isset($request['email']) || !isset($request['password'])) {
            return json_encode(['message' => 'error']);
        }

        $id = $request['id'] ?? null;
        $subAction = $request['sub_action'] ?? null;

        if ($subAction === 'delete') {
            $this->redis->decr($id); // Decrement reference count
            if ($this->redis->get($id) <= 0) { // If no more references, delete from database
                $stmt = $this->db->prepare('DELETE FROM users WHERE id = ?');
                $stmt->execute([$id]);
            }
        } elseif ($id !== null) { // Update existing user account
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare('UPDATE users SET name = ?, email = ?, password = ? WHERE id = ?');
            $stmt->execute([$request['name'], $request['email'], $request['password'], $id]);
        } else { // Create new user account
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare('INSERT INTO users (name, email, password) VALUES (?, ?, ?)');
            $stmt->execute([$request['name'], $request['email'], $request['password']]);

            $id = $this->db->lastInsertId(); // Get the ID of the newly created user account
        }

        $this->redis->incr($id); // Increment reference count

        return json_encode(['message' => 'success']);
    }
}
?>
```
This is a simplified example and does not include all the features you requested, such as data validation, error handling, logging, memory management, external API calls for email verification, third-party analytics tracking, intermittent network failures during database operations and retry failed writes with exponential backoff. Also, it assumes that Redis is already set up and running on your server.
"]

