['Here is an example of how you might implement such a function in C using pthreads for thread safety and locking. This code does not include all the optimizations mentioned in your request as they would make the solution too complex to be presented here. However, it provides a good starting point for understanding how to handle concurrency and memory management in a high-performance network application:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct UEContextInfo {
    int UE_ID;
    int RRC_STATE;
    int ServingCellID;
    int LastReceivedPDUSequenceNumber;
} UEContextInfo;

typedef struct UEContextArray {
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    UEContextInfo* contexts;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} UEContextArray;

UEContextArray* createUEContextArray() {
    UEContextArray* array = malloc(sizeof(UEContextArray));
    pthread_mutex_init(&array->lock, NULL);
    array->count = 0;
    array->capacity = 1024;  // Start with a reasonable initial capacity.
    array->contexts = malloc(sizeof(UEContextInfo) * array->capacity);
    return array;
}

void addUEContext(UEContextArray* array, UEContextInfo context) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array->lock);

    if (array->count == array->capacity) {
        // Double the capacity when full. This is a simple strategy for handling dynamic resizing.
        array->capacity *= 2;
        array->contexts = realloc(array->contexts, sizeof(UEContextInfo) * array->capacity);
    }

    array->contexts[array->count++] = context;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array->lock);
}

void removeUEContext(UEContextArray* array, int UE_ID) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array->lock);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < array->count; ++i) {
        if (array->contexts[i].UE_ID == UE_ID) {
            // Move the last context to this position and decrement count.
            array->contexts[i] = array->contexts[--array->count];
            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array->lock);
}
```
This code uses a simple strategy for handling dynamic resizing of the UEContextArray. When the array is full, it doubles its capacity by reallocating memory. This ensures that adding new contexts has amortized constant time complexity. However, this approach can lead to high memory usage if many contexts are added and removed in succession.

The code also uses a mutex for thread safety. This ensures that only one thread can modify the array at a time, preventing race conditions and ensuring data consistency. However, it does not handle cases where multiple threads need to access different parts of the array concurrently. For this, you would need to use more complex locking strategies or consider using a lock-free data structure.
']

