["Here is a basic implementation in C using pthreads for thread safety and LRU cache for managing list size. This code does not handle system dependencies or performance constraints mentioned in the task description. It also doesn't include error checking, which would be necessary in a production environment. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
    int CRCFLAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* prev;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
Node* tail = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;
int count = 0;

void add(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* node = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (node == NULL) {
        // Handle memory allocation failure
        return;
    }

    node->data = data;
    node->prev = NULL;
    node->next = head;

    if (head != NULL) {
        head->prev = node;
    } else {
        tail = node;
    }

    head = node;
    count++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

int getDistinctRNTI() {
    int distinct = 0;
    Node* current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        distinct++;
        current = current->next;
    }

    return distinct;
}

void removeLeastRecentlyUsed(int limit) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* current = tail;
    while (count > limit && current != NULL) {
        count--;
        if (current->prev != NULL) {
            current->prev->next = NULL;
        } else {
            head = NULL;
        }

        free(current);
        current = tail;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code provides a basic implementation of the requirements. It uses a doubly linked list to manage the `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures, with each node containing a `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structure and pointers to the previous and next nodes in the list. The `add()` function adds new elements to the head of the list, while the `removeLeastRecentlyUsed()` function removes elements from the tail of the list if the size limit is exceeded.

The code also uses a mutex lock for thread safety when adding and removing elements from the list. This ensures that only one thread can access the list at a time, preventing race conditions.

