int totalMemory = 0;
const int MAX_MEMORY = 1024 * 1024; // 1MB

void addUE(int id, char* state) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    UE_INSTANCE *newNode = (UE_INSTANCE*) malloc(sizeof(UE_INSTANCE));
    if (!newNode || totalMemory + sizeof(UE_INSTANCE) > MAX_MEMORY) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        printf("Error: Memory allocation failed or maximum memory limit exceeded.\n");
        return;
    }

    newNode->id = id;
    newNode->state = state;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;
    totalMemory += sizeof(UE_INSTANCE);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void removeUE(int id) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    UE_INSTANCE *current = head, *prev = NULL;
    while (current != NULL && current->id != id) {
        prev = current;
        current = current->next;
    }

    if (current == NULL) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        printf("Error: UE with the given ID not found.\n");
        return;
    }

    if (prev != NULL) 
        prev->next = current->next;
    else 
        head = current->next;

    totalMemory -= sizeof(UE_INSTANCE);
    free(current);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a linked list to manage the UE instances. Each UE instance is represented by a node in the list, which contains an ID and state. The addUE function adds a new UE instance at the head of the list, while the removeUE function removes an existing UE instance based on its ID. 

The code also uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when adding or removing UE instances from the list. This prevents data corruption that could occur if multiple threads attempt to modify the list concurrently. 

Finally, the code checks whether memory allocation for a new UE instance would exceed the maximum memory limit before attempting to allocate memory. If it does, an error message is printed and no further action is taken.
